  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Given the function $f(x)=4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4(-\\frac{1}{2})+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2198$ | min | $\u2197$ |  | $\u2197$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
